3.8 Cross-border African use case
Imagine an AfCFTA-oriented digital trade platform.

A company wants to participate in cross-border commerce.

The platform requests:
Business credential
Tax credential
Trade license
Authorized representative credential

The merchant presents the credentials.

The platform verifies:
Issuer authenticity
+
Digital signature
+
Credential integrity
+
Credential status
+
Presentation integrity
+
Business policy

Then:
         VERIFIED
             ↓
       Trade account
             ↓
      Access to market

3.9 Privacy by design
Students should avoid designing systems that expose unnecessary personal information.

Suppose a merchant only needs to prove:
"I am a registered business."

The verifier may not need:
- owner's full residential address;
- unrelated identity documents;
- personal phone records;
- complete tax history.
The principle is:
Prove what is necessary, disclose what is necessary.

This is especially important when designing national or cross-border identity infrastructure.
3.10 Credential lifecycle
A credential should have a lifecycle:
ISSUE
  ↓
STORE
  ↓
PRESENT
  ↓
VERIFY
  ↓
USE
  ↓
REVOKE / EXPIRE

Students must therefore consider credential status.

A credential that was valid yesterday may no longer be valid today.
